# What never reaches the Docker daemon.
#
# The Dockerfile copies `accountant/` and nothing else, so most of this file is
# not what makes the image small - it is what makes a FUTURE `COPY . .` safe.
# The one-line change that turns a careful copy into a whole-tree copy is the
# commonest way a secret, a database or a virtualenv ends up in a published
# layer, and a .dockerignore is the only guard that survives that edit.
#
# It also stops the build context being sent at all: without the `.git` and
# `.venv` lines below, every build uploads hundreds of megabytes to the daemon
# before reading a single instruction.

# THE DURABLE DATABASE. A real customer's audit trail, their vendor history and
# their account history. .gitignore already refuses to commit it; this refuses
# to build it into an image that then goes to a registry. Same file, second
# door.
data/
*.db
*.sqlite3

# Virtualenvs. Platform-specific binaries compiled for the developer's machine,
# and the single largest thing in the tree.
.venv/
venv/

# Bytecode and tool caches. Stale, host-specific, and never wanted in an image.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.testmondata*
.gremlins_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage.xml
coverage/
htmlcov/

# History and CI configuration. `.git` in particular carries every version of
# every file ever committed, including anything later removed from the tip.
.git/
.github/
.gitignore
.pre-commit-config.yaml

# Nothing here runs in production.
tests/
ci/
docs/
scripts/
artifacts/
.audit/
reports/
dist/
build/
.tools/

# Build and deployment definitions themselves. They describe the image; they do
# not belong inside it.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore

# Editor and OS leavings.
.DS_Store
